{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019dqJRZej6Hbvk48XF828JL","type":"tool_result","content":"===== SR-174\r\n  title: Work-item identity is allocated once and never re-issued\r\n  requirement: The delivered loop content shall allocate each work-item identity at most once, so that no two concurrent actors receive the same identity and an identity freed by a deletion is never re-issued.\r\n  acceptance_criteria: Two actors finishing against the same tree cannot obtain the same work-item identity; an identity freed by deleting its record is not re-issued to new work; allocation counts from the recorded high-water mark rather than from the live maximum.\r\n  hat_refs: ['INTEGRITY-RECOVERABILITY', 'MAINTAINER']\r\n  rationale: Identity allocation is its own decision, not a consequence of serialization: serializing the writer makes a COLLISION unlikely and does nothing about RE-USE, which is the half that silently re-points every commit message and archived document citing the freed id. The non-reuse clause has a `shall` of its own here rather than living in SR-170's acceptance, which is a cell that cannot mint a requirement. Contributes to SN-025 (the ready frontier ordered deterministically, so two readers of the same registry dispatch the same work) by supplying a work-item identity that names one record for every reader, before and after a deletion.\r\n===== SR-228\r\n  title: Held-rung CLARITY re-attestation\r\n  requirement: Where the declared approval level holds a tier for a human, the delivered loop shall permit an independent adjudication to re-attest an approved amended row only when its verdict rules that amendment CLARITY.\r\n  acceptance_criteria: On a held tier, an adjudication act re-attesting a row is refused unless its recorded verdict names that row as CLARITY; a MEANING verdict and an absent, unreadable or non-CLARITY verdict are refused by row; the recorded act names the verdict for later audit; and a first draft is not re-attested under this allowance.\r\n  hat_refs: ['SECURITY']\r\n  rationale: SN-029 delegates review on released tiers without skipping it, but an approved row whose wording is clarified on a held tier remains anchored to a signature the owner already gave. Treating that unchanged meaning as a new approval would strand every clarification with the owner; treating a meaning change the same way would carry a signature onto text the owner did not read. The verdict boundary keeps those alternatives separate: only the independent judgement that the signature still describes the row permits re-attestation, while a meaning change remains the owner's decision.\r\n===== SR-178\r\n  title: Text that has moved away from its acceptance record is reported\r\n  requirement: The kit shall report any recorded artifact whose text has moved away from the\r\ncopy recording its acceptance - stakeholder needs included - regardless of any\r\nStatus movement.\r\n  acceptance_criteria: An artifact whose normative text has moved since the copy that recorded its acceptance is reported whether or not its Status moved; an approved cell moving under a row still claiming approval is reported, a merely traced cell moving is not, and a row below approval has no claim to fall from and is never reported; the status marker itself is never read as the movement, so a flip is neither an amendment nor a mask for one; and stakeholder needs, assumptions and surrogates are held to the same rule.\r\n  hat_refs: ['TEST-ENGINEER']\r\n  rationale: THE DRIFT half, separable from SR-140 in the direction that matters: a record that exists and rides its approval commit, with nothing ever comparing live text against it, satisfies SR-140 completely and leaves every later amendment invisible. That is not a hypothetical - it is precisely the failure the git-history derivation had, and the reason a baseline on disk was owed at all.\r\nREGARDLESS OF STATUS MOVEMENT is the load-bearing clause, not a qualifier. The amendment machinery watches the Status flip, and the sanctioned staged-amendment path deliberately does not flip - so a rule keyed off the flip returns nothing on exactly
[... byte cap hit — full stream in out/run-logs/ ...]
58 Detail -> before: one two-tree walk feeds the staged-amendment readers, and the amend-without-flip warn and the intake amendment mint both consume that walk over the full AMENDMENT_CSVS universe -> after: the same, plus: the mint additionally drops records the merged range already settled under LLR-342, while the pre-commit warn reads the unfiltered walk because it runs before any act -> not the same: it fixes a difference between the walk's two consumers (one filtered, one not) that the old text treated as identical, and it pins the warn to the unfiltered walk. Ready to bless. The sentence sits beside the consumer statement it qualifies and keeps the shared-walk invariant: the filter is applied after the walk, by the mint alone, so the one definition of movement and the exempt-versus-report symmetry the row exists for still hold. Its reason (\"runs before any act\") is one a reader can check: a pre-commit warn has no act ledger entry to settle against. It defers the filter's rule to LLR-342 and names no new implementation symbol. Downward, TC-373 holds the mint-side filter and TC-153 the warn's unchanged reading.\n\nVERDICT: MEANING rows=2\n\n## first-approval\n\nBoth rows are ready, and I approve both. I read them against SR-178 above, the two amended siblings, the four approved LLRs and five approved TCs in the chain, the WI-963 Done-when, and the lane's arms map. All fourteen tests TC-373 cites exist in `tests/test_intake_settled.py`, and its missing-copy arm is parametrized over both shapes (an absent copy, a copy without the row).\n\n- [APPROVE] LLR-342 -> the obligation: when the amendment walk returns records, the trunk mint excludes a record only when both hold: an act-ledger entry the requested range added re-attests the row, and the row's whole approved and routed traced cell sets at the range's after revision equal its approved-snapshot copy there, cells outside the net range diff included. A tree that does not list the ledger supplies no acts. A missing copy, or a copy without the row, leaves the record to mint. A listed ledger or copy that cannot be read or does not parse refuses the mint, naming the carrier and the revision, and is never treated as absent. One filtering result serves the post-merge and the range-sweep routes. The refusal states that the merge stands and names two recovery routes (a fetch then a same-range sweep; a reviewed repair then a sweep whose after revision is the repair). The contract covers supported routes' carriers, and hand edits, aliases and sub-second windows are outside it -> what its chain shows: SR-178 reports text that has moved away from its acceptance copy. A row re-attested in the range and equal cell for cell to that copy has not moved away from it, so dropping its record is SR-178's own boundary, not a carve-out. The spec anchors the filter there and allowed a new LLR under it. LLR-153 and LLR-158 each point at this row instead of restating it, so the settled set has one home. LLR-278 already bounds which re-attestations can enter the ledger at merge, so the authority this row leans on is the one verdicts already gate. TC-373 verifies every arm, each clause matching a row of the arms map -> why it is ready: every clause is an observable input, outcome or refusal a builder could fail. The two-condition exclusion is fail-closed in each direction a mistake could hide an amendment: no act, a later amendment, a restored cell, a moved routed traced cell, or a missing or rowless copy all still mint, and an unreadable carrier refuses rather than reads as absent. The Rationale argues exactly those two failure classes. The text names only boundary artifacts (the act ledger, the approved-snapshot copy) and registry ids, with no code symbol. It has no `shall`. It carries no Hat-Refs, which is right, since SR-178's TEST-ENGINEER already covers the drift-reporting failure this row serves. The coverage bound mirrors PROCESS.md §6's threat model in the spec's own words.\n- [APPROVE] TC-373 -> the obligation: drive the settled-amendment filter on real repositories and assert:\n  - settled records are omitted on both mint routes;\n  - later or unrecorded amendments, and copies that do not prove settlement, still mint;\n  - an absent ledger changes nothing, and the filter is not consulted without records;\n  - a listed unreadable or unparseable ledger or copy refuses by name and mints nothing;\n  - the whole-row comparison catches a restored cell and a routed traced cell moved after the act, while an unrouted traced cell moved after the act still settles;\n  - the two replayed real landings mint nothing.\n\n  -> what its chain shows: it verifies SR-178, LLR-342 and IF-091. Each LLR-342 arm has a named case: both routes; the range-added act and the whole-cell equality; absent versus listed carriers; refusal by carrier; missing or rowless copy; recovery wording. Its routed-versus-unrouted contrast pins the cell set LLR-342 states to the routed traced cells LLR-153 already defines. The real-landing replay answers the Done-when's WI-926 and WI-956 regression, and Full is the honest tier for a module of real git fixtures -> why it is ready: Expected states the condition (SR-178's criterion, held through LLR-342's contract and IF-091's call outcome) rather than an instrument. Automated, Level and Tier are unambiguous. Nothing LLR-342 says goes unverified. One note, not a finding: the Method repeats the Evidence cell's test names before stating its assertions. That is a second copy that can go stale. It follows the precedent of approved TC-153 and TC-331, and the assertion half stands on its own, so it does not hold the row back.\n\nOUTCOME: APPROVE rows=2\n\nSITTING: JUDGED kinds=amendment;first-approval\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2173,"cache_read_input_tokens":235879,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"eph